    {
      "slug": "he-presented-my-idea-as-his-own",
      "script": "I spent three weeks building a pitch deck for a client renewal, color-coded, backed by data, the works. That morning, my coworker grabbed my files off the shared drive and opened the deck under his own name before I arrived. He presented it line by line, confident, smiling, while I sat behind him. Then the client asked a follow-up about the churn model on slide nine. He froze. The dataset was mine, and he had never opened the methodology tab. After thirty seconds, I raised my hand and answered it in under a minute, citing the exact spreadsheet. The client thanked me by name. My manager pulled him aside, and within the week the account was reassigned to me, full credit, full commission. He stole the deck; the questions returned it.",
      "editor_note": "Compressed the story to 124 words, preserved every protected fact and visual phrase, and sharpened the payoff around the stolen work proving its owner."
    },
    {
      "slug": "she-threatened-a-1-star-review",
      "script": "A woman stormed up to my register holding a half-eaten sandwich and demanded a full refund plus a free meal, claiming she found a hair before two bites. I offered a standard refund. She refused, raised her phone, and threatened to post a one-star review with photos unless I comped her next five visits. I said policy required me to check the kitchen cameras first for any goodwill comp over ten dollars. She agreed, smirking. The footage showed her pulling a hair from her own ponytail, then tucking it under the lettuce thirty seconds before calling me over. I printed a single frame, slid it across the counter, and asked whether she still wanted to post. She left without the refund, the free meal, or the review. Turns out the camera had the strongest review.",
      "editor_note": "Compressed the story to 135 words, preserved every protected fact and visual phrase, and gave the camera reveal a concise review callback."
    }
  ],
