    {
      "slug": "cereal-production-is-1-5x-higher-than-in-1972",
      "title": "The Food Shortage Forecast Missed The Harvest",
      "hook": "The world was told food would run out. The data took a very different route.",
      "says": [
        "In 1985, progression to secondary school (%) was 77.8. By 2017, it was 91.3 — 1.2 times higher.",
        "In 1972, cereal production was 1083.7 million metric tons. By 2024, it was 1644.1 million metric tons — 1.5 times higher.",
        "China leads at 5.2 million square kilometers of farmland, then the United States at 4.2 million, Australia at 3.6 million, Brazil at 2.4 million, and Russia at 2.2 million."
      ],
      "closing": "The feared famine never arrived the way people predicted. School climbed, grain climbed, and the forecast missed the field.",
      "question": "Which number surprised you most — school, grain, or land?"
    },
    {
      "slug": "combustible-renewables-and-waste-is-3-0x-higher-than-in-1990",
      "title": "Waste Energy Tripled—And It Isn’t Solar",
      "hook": "One energy source tripled. It is mostly the stuff people throw away.",
      "says": [
        "In 1990, combustible renewables and waste was 10.2 % of total energy. By 2024, it was 30.1 % of total energy — 3.0 times higher.",
        "In 1998, access to electricity was 73.2 % of population. By 2024, it was 91.9 % of population — 1.3 times higher.",
        "In 1972, methane (ch4) emissions from waste was 903.8 Mt CO2e. By 2024, it was 1,652 Mt CO2e — 1.8 times higher."
      ],
      "closing": "A third of the energy mix now comes from waste and renewables. Useful trash has had quite a career.",
      "question": "Which number surprised you most — fuel, power, or waste?"
    },
    {
      "slug": "population-growth-fell-54-since-1972",
      "title": "Population Growth Didn’t Keep Accelerating",
      "hook": "The population curve changed direction. Quietly, but dramatically.",
      "says": [
        "In 1972, rural population was 63.1 % of total population. By 2025, it was 42.2 % of total population — down to 67 percent of that.",
        "In 1972, population in the largest city was 18.2 % of urban population. By 2025, it was 16.2 % of urban population — barely moved.",
        "In 1972, population growth was 2 annual %. By 2025, it was 0.91 annual % — down to 46 percent of that."
      ],
      "closing": "The planet is still growing. What faded was the acceleration everyone assumed would keep going.",
      "question": "Which number surprised you most — rural, city, or growth?"
    }
  ],
